𝐃𝐞𝐯𝐎𝐩𝐬 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐜𝐞𝐭𝐩𝐬:

❓𝐘𝐨𝐮 𝐡𝐚𝐯𝐞 𝐚𝐧 𝐚𝐩𝐩𝐥𝐢𝐜𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐫𝐮𝐧𝐧𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐨𝐧 𝐚𝐧 𝐄𝐂2 𝐢𝐧𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞 𝐭𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐧𝐞𝐞𝐝𝐬 𝐭𝐨 𝐚𝐜𝐜𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐚𝐧 𝐒3 𝐛𝐮𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐭. 𝐇𝐨𝐰 𝐰𝐨𝐮𝐥𝐝 𝐲𝐨𝐮 𝐜𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐫𝐞 𝐭𝐡𝐢𝐬 𝐚𝐜𝐜𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐬𝐞𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐞𝐥𝐲 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐞𝐟𝐟𝐢𝐜𝐢𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐥𝐲.

🛡️ Step-by-Step: Secure & Efficient EC2 → S3 Access

🧩𝐂𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐞 𝐚𝐧 𝐈𝐀𝐌 𝐑𝐨𝐥𝐞 𝐟𝐨𝐫 𝐄𝐂2

Go to IAM → Roles → Create Role.

Trusted entity: EC2.

Attach policy: Use a managed or custom policy like:

{
 "Version": "2012-10-17",
 "Statement": [
  {
   "Effect": "Allow",
   "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
   "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name/*"]
  }
 ]
}

🎯𝐀𝐭𝐭𝐚𝐜𝐡 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐈𝐀𝐌 𝐑𝐨𝐥𝐞 𝐭𝐨 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐄𝐂2 𝐈𝐧𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞

Go to EC2 → Instances → Select your instance.

Actions → Security → Modify IAM Role → Attach EC2S3AccessRole.

✅ No need to store AWS credentials or access keys on the instance.

🔍 𝐀𝐜𝐜𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐒3 𝐟𝐫𝐨𝐦 𝐘𝐨𝐮𝐫 𝐀𝐩𝐩𝐥𝐢𝐜𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧

If you're using the AWS SDK or CLI, it will automatically use the instance role:

aws s3 cp /tmp/data.txt s3://your-bucket-name/data.txt

🔐 𝐒𝐞𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐞 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐒3 𝐁𝐮𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐭

Use bucket policies to restrict access to only the EC2 role:

{
 "Effect": "Allow",
 "Principal": {
  "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/EC2S3AccessRole"
 },
 "Action": "s3:*",
 "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name/*"]
}

• Enable bucket encryption (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS).
• Enable access logging and versioning for audit and recovery.

🌟 Monitor & Audit

• Use CloudTrail to track S3 access events.
• Use IAM Access Analyzer to detect overly permissive policies.
• Set up CloudWatch Alarms for unusual activity.

💡 Summary:
Use an IAM role attached to your EC2 instance to securely access the S3 bucket without storing credentials. Restrict permissions via IAM and bucket policies and monitor access using CloudTrail and CloudWatch.
